WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.370
Film Courage: Chris I want to hear about you
taking this week long drive across country?

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.240
Chris von Hoffmann, writer/director: It was
like 4 or 5 days?

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.240
Film Courage: Okay.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.930
You said you didn’t totally plan it out
to come to LA?

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.590
You had been thinking about it for a long
time or…?

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.400
Chris von Hoffmann: I had been thinking about
it since I was in high school.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.560
I mean I had been wanting to move out here
for acting at the time.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:25.740
But at a certain point I was like ‘I think
I’ve done all that I can do in New York.’

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:32.720
I mean I was hardcore wanting to pursue filmmaking
at the time and New York just was so theater

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.470
driven and I love theater, I love Broadway
and all of that.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:40.170
I love that stuff and I wanted to be an actor
on stage for a long time.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:48.309
As far as the film business, there is a lot
of film that shoots out there but LA is just

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:54.140
is the capital of movies.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:01:05.439
And so I just thought I’ve just got 
to do it.

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:06.790
I recommend it to a lot people.

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:08.840
I mean a lot people fly their cars out.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.310
Film Courage: You drove?

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:19.549
Chris von Hoffmann: Yeah, through Oklahoma
and New Mexico and stayed at these cheap motels

00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:25.079
and these weird places…like Scottsdale,
Arizona and I got to see all of these places.

00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:27.579
Film Courage: Did you see real-life drifters?

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:30.299
[Chris is writer/director of the horror film
DRIFTER].

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:31.659
Chris von Hoffmann: Probably?

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:32.990
I mean it was like 2012.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:36.259
I came across some weird people, yeah.

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:40.200
Just in different corners of the town.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.429
But I definitely recommend it.

00:01:42.429 --> 00:01:46.200
I loved the experience, I definitely recommend
it.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.289
I hated pulling over for gas or pulling over
to use the restroom because I love to keep

00:01:50.289 --> 00:01:53.060
moving, going.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:59.130
It was a very transcending, very therapeutic
experience because you’re not thinking about

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.389
what’s ahead of you just yet.

00:02:00.389 --> 00:02:02.819
You’re not thinking about the past at all.

00:02:02.819 --> 00:02:06.799
And it’s such like an in-moment kind of
thing.

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:14.140
It was great and one of the best experiences
of my life driving out to LA.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:25.239
You’re not thinking about anything, there’s
nothing really hardcore to think about yet

00:02:25.239 --> 00:02:31.380
in that tiny amount of time and you’re sort
of just going moment-to-moment and then you

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:36.840
move to LA and you’re like oh ‘Now I’ve
got to get back to reality’ and then it

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.549
gets gruesome.

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:42.060
But that brief amount of time was great.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.459
Film Courage: Were there things leading up
to that moment where you said “Okay, I think

00:02:46.459 --> 00:02:48.990
now is the time,” you were 24 you say?

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:55.189
Chris von Hoffmann: I think making that short
probably and then I remember thinking ‘Should

00:02:55.189 --> 00:03:00.909
I not make this short and move out to LA blindly?

00:03:00.909 --> 00:03:05.329
Or should I wait a few months, make the short
and then move out to LA?’

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:08.049
That felt like the best bet to do something
like that because I didn’t want to go out

00:03:08.049 --> 00:03:11.069
there with literally nothing.

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:18.030
And so I just made the short and then screened
it at this place called The Producer’s Club

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.030
in New York City (which is on 44th street).

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:24.409
It’s a little blackbox, they do a lot of
rehearsals and plays there, but they also

00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:28.530
do screenings and they have a projector that
you can rent.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:32.549
And 70 people came to it, so it was a nice
little turn out for that movie and they responded

00:03:32.549 --> 00:03:38.731
to it very well and so that boosted up your
confidence and then you go to LA and all of

00:03:38.731 --> 00:03:41.749
your confidence and ego is just shattered
within minutes so…

00:03:41.749 --> 00:03:43.889
Film Courage: Every day of every year.

00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:49.270
Chris von Hoffmann: Every day is just like
constantly cutthroat and brutal.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:57.970
I think it was great to have that short film
in my back pocket because that helped me to

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:03.760
meet other people for later for other shorts
and they could take me somewhat seriously

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.319
that I’m not a total idiot, that I can make
movies.

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:13.800
And then I start to make more and more shorts
with more and more people I was meeting out

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:21.630
here and just honing the craft and learning
just about the nuts and bolts of movies.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:26.370
Film Courage: Was there a moment that you
thought about turning the car around?

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:29.889
Chris von Hoffmann: No.

00:04:29.889 --> 00:04:38.170
It was a pretty straight shoot just like there
is no turning back.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:43.639
I was just at the point in my life where I
was like there was nothing more for me to

00:04:43.639 --> 00:04:45.910
do out in New York.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:49.500
I know what I want to do right now, I know
this path I want to take and New York is just

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.280
not going to do it for me.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:57.380
And if I’m serious about this and serious
about pursuing directing and wanting to be

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:00.610
a writer/director then I have to move to Los
Angeles.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:04.520
And I remember one of the first things is
when I moved out to LA I went to a screening

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:09.539
of SLITHER (James Gunn’s movie, one of his
early movies) and I went to The Aero Theatre

00:05:09.539 --> 00:05:14.061
(it was the first time I went there) and James
Gunn was there for a Q&amp;A and the first thing

00:05:14.061 --> 00:05:16.810
that came out of his mouth was “You have
to move to Los Angeles.”

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:23.060
And I was like “Well, it’s been validated
(now I feel good about this).”

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:29.919
I know some people live in Toronto and try
to do film in New York and all of that and

00:05:29.919 --> 00:05:32.080
I guess there is work all over the place.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.229
But LA is the heart and soul of it and it’s
just so much more convenient for everybody

00:05:36.229 --> 00:05:41.879
and all the deals are made out here and everyone
is in sync.

00:05:41.879 --> 00:05:47.430
It is like a bubble and it’s good to be
part of that bubble I think.

